---
title: "Layout: where a section begins and ends"
bibliography: references.bib
---

Blocks are flat siblings of one root. Nothing wraps a heading and the content beneath
it, which is what makes the incremental block swap cheap: every block is replaceable on
its own, with no container to re-parent. The cost is that the DOM could not say where a
section *stopped*, so anything wanting to measure or fold one had to re-derive the
boundaries by walking tag names.

Each heading now carries `data-section-end`, naming the `data-block-id` of the last
block its section covers. This page is that rule written down as the shapes it has to
survive.

## A section with content beneath it {#sec-plain}

The ordinary case. This paragraph and the one after it belong to the section opened
above, so the heading's `data-section-end` names the last of them.

Extents are inclusive of the heading itself, so a section is never empty and a consumer
never needs a missing-value case.

## A section that contains subsections {#sec-nested}

Extents nest. This `##` section does not stop at the `###` below it: it stops where the
next section at its own level begins, so its extent covers every subsection too.

That direction is the one that keeps information. The flat heading-to-next-heading run
is recoverable from wherever the next heading sits; the nesting is not recoverable from
the flat runs.

### A subsection {#sec-nested-first}

The subsection's own extent stops at its sibling below.

### A second subsection {#sec-nested-second}

And this one stops where the next `##` begins, which is also where its parent stops.
Two extents ending on the same block is normal, not a collision.

## A heading with nothing beneath it {#sec-empty}

## The heading above has an empty section {#sec-after-empty}

The `## A heading with nothing beneath it` section has no body at all: the next heading
follows it immediately. Its extent is its own block id, which is what "inclusive of the
heading" buys, and it is the shape a document acquires the moment an author writes two
headings before filling the first one in.

## A section that runs to the end of the document {#sec-last}

The last section has no following heading to stop at, so it ends at the last block of
the *body*. Generated document furniture is appended after that: this page cites a
source [@tufte1983], and the References section it produces is not part of any section.
It is a structure the document grew, not content the author filed under a heading.

A footnote[^extent] does *not* produce furniture, which is the distinction worth having
on this page: its note renders beside its own reference, inside this very paragraph, so
it is part of the last section rather than appended after it.

[^extent]: This note is inside the block that references it, not in a list at the end of
the page, so it moves nothing about where the section stops.
